Enforcement Decree of the Act on Fostering and Supporting the Fishery Food Industry

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on Fostering and Supporting the Fishery Food Industry and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Scope of the Fishery Food Industry)
"Industries prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on Fostering and Supporting the Fishery Food Industry (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means the following industries:
1. Industries that produce, process, manufacture, or cook products by processing fishery products;
2. Industries that package, store, transport, or sell products produced by the industries under subparagraph 1.

CHAPTER II ESTABLISHMENT OF A FOUNDATION FOR FOSTERING THE FISHERY FOOD INDUSTRY

Article 3 (Minor Changes)
"Minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso of Article 5 (2) and the proviso of Article 13 (4) of the Act respectively means a simple mistake, clerical error, omission, or comparable obvious error.

Article 4 (Formulation of Implementation Plans for Fostering the Fishery Food Industry)
The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall formulate an annual implementation plan by November 30 each year pursuant to Article 5 (4) of the Act.

Article 5 (Designation of Institutions for Training Specialized Personnel)
(1) Pursuant to Article 6 (2) of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as a "Mayor/Do Governor") may designate an institution for training specialized personnel for the fishery food industry (hereinafter referred to as a "fishery food personnel training institution") from among the following institutions or organizations:
1. An educational institution that establishes and operates curricula related to fishery food or a research institution that conducts research on fishery food;
2. A fishery food business operators' organization established pursuant to Article 11 of the Act;
3. An institution related to the fishery food industry among public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
4. An institution related to the fishery food industry among government-funded science and technology research institutes under Article 8 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Science and Technology Research Institutes;
5. A nonprofit corporation related to the fishery food industry established pursuant to Article 32 of the Civil Act;
6. An institution related to the fishery food industry among government-funded research institutes under Article 8 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes;
7. A private teaching institute registered pursuant to Article 6 of the Act on the Establishment and Operation of Private Teaching Institutes and Extracurricular Lessons to provide specialized instruction in techniques for producing, processing, manufacturing, or cooking fishery food, etc.;
8. Other institutions or organizations established for the purpose of conducting business related to fishery food.
(2) An institution or organization seeking designation as a fishery food personnel training institution shall meet all of the following requirements:
1. It shall have an organization, personnel, facilities, etc. responsible for education and training;
2. Its articles of incorporation or equivalent business operation regulations shall specify the training of specialized personnel related to the fishery food industry as a principal business.
(3) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or a Mayor/Do Governor intends to designate a fishery food personnel training institution pursuant to Article 6 (2) of the Act, the Minister or Mayor/Do Governor shall publicly announce in advance the application period and other matters necessary for applications for designation.
(4) An institution or organization seeking designation as a fishery food personnel training institution shall submit an application for designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the Mayor/Do Governor, together with the following documents:
1. The current status of educational facilities held;
2. The current status of secured relevant personnel, including instructors;
3. An operation plan including curricula and educational content;
4. A plan for financing operating expenses;
5. Articles of incorporation or equivalent business operation regulations.
(5) When the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or a Mayor/Do Governor designates a fishery food personnel training institution pursuant to Article 6 (2) of the Act, the Minister or Mayor/Do Governor shall issue a certificate of designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(6) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or a Mayor/Do Governor designates a fishery food personnel training institution pursuant to Article 6 (2) of the Act or revokes its designation pursuant to paragraph (4) of that Article, the Minister or Mayor/Do Governor shall publicly announce that fact.
(7) A fishery food personnel training institution may provide education and training on the following matters to train specialized personnel:
1. Techniques for manufacturing, processing, and cooking fishery food;
2. Management of the quality, nutrition, and hygiene of fishery food;
3. Management and establishment of businesses related to fishery food;
4. Adaptation training for foreigners working or intending to work in the fishery food industry;
5. Other matters that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the Mayor/Do Governor deems necessary for fostering the fishery food industry.
(8) A fishery food personnel training institution shall report its business plan for the relevant year and its business performance for the preceding year to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the Mayor/Do Governor by January 31 each year.
(9) The criteria for revocation of the designation of a fishery food personnel training institution under Article 6 (4) of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 1.
(10) Matters necessary for the designation, operation, etc. of fishery food personnel training institutions, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (9), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the Mayor/Do Governor.

Article 6 (Support for Expenses)
Pursuant to Article 6 (3) of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the head of a local government may provide a fishery food personnel training institution with support for all or part of the following expenses necessary for training specialized personnel:
1. Costs necessary for developing educational programs and developing and providing related materials, etc.;
2. Costs necessary for operating educational programs, such as instructors' lecture fees and allowances;
3. Costs of purchasing educational equipment and materials and installing and operating educational facilities;
4. Costs necessary for surveys and research to train specialized personnel;
5. Domestic and overseas training expenses to enhance the expertise of staff and trainees of fishery food personnel training institutions;
6. Other expenses that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the head of the local government deems necessary for training specialized personnel.

Article 7 (Designation of Specialized Institutions for Fishery Food Industry Information Analysis, etc.)
(1) An institution, organization, or corporation seeking designation as a specialized institution for fishery food industry information analysis under Article 8 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "specialized fishery food industry information analysis institution") shall meet all of the following requirements:
1. It shall have at least two personnel with experience in performing fishery food industry information analysis duties;
2. Fishery food industry information analysis duties shall be prescribed as a principal business in its articles of incorporation or equivalent business operating regulations.
(2) Before designating a specialized fishery food industry information analysis institution under Article 8 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall publicly announce the application period for designation and other matters necessary for applying for designation.
(3) An institution, organization, or corporation seeking designation as a specialized fishery food industry information analysis institution shall submit to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries an application for designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, accompanied by the following documents:
1. The current status of personnel;
2. A plan for implementing, operating, and managing its business;
3. Its record of research or business related to fishery food industry information analysis;
4. Its articles of incorporation or equivalent business operating regulations.
(4) Upon designating a specialized fishery food industry information analysis institution under Article 8 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall issue a certificate of designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(5) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries designates a specialized fishery food industry information analysis institution under Article 8 (1) of the Act or revokes its designation under paragraph (4) of that Article, the Minister shall publicly announce that fact.
(6) A specialized fishery food industry information analysis institution designated under Article 8 (1) of the Act shall submit an annual report to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries within 60 days after the end of each business year.
(7) The criteria for revoking the designation of a specialized fishery food industry information analysis institution under Article 8 (4) of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 2.
(8) Matters necessary for the designation and operation of specialized fishery food industry information analysis institutions, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (7), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 8 (Matters to Be Included in Articles of Incorporation of Fishery Food Business Operators' Organizations, etc.)
(1) The articles of incorporation of a fishery food business operators' organization established under Article 11 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "fishery food business operators' organization") shall include the following matters:
1. Its purpose;
2. Its name;
3. The address of its principal office;
4. Matters concerning assets;
5. Matters concerning the appointment and removal of directors;
6. Matters concerning the qualifications, admission, and withdrawal of members;
7. Matters concerning the general meeting and board of directors;
8. Where grounds for dissolution are prescribed, matters concerning those grounds.
(2) A fishery food business operators' organization shall submit the following documents to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries within 60 days after the end of each business year:
1. A business plan and a budget of revenues and expenditures for the following business year;
2. A report on business performance and a statement of settled revenues and expenditures for the relevant business year;
3. An inventory of property as of the end of the relevant business year.
(3) Matters necessary for the operation and supervision of fishery food business operators' organizations, other than those prescribed in paragraph (2), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 9 (Designation of Institutions Supporting Fishery Food Exports, etc.)
(1) "Relevant institutions, organizations, or corporations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 12 (1) of the Act means institutions, organizations, or corporations meeting all of the following requirements:
1. They shall have personnel with experience in conducting the projects specified in the subparagraphs of Article 12 (2) of the Act;
2. The projects specified in the subparagraphs of Article 12 (2) of the Act shall be prescribed as a principal business in their articles of incorporation or equivalent business operating regulations.
(2) When designating an institution supporting fishery food exports under Article 12 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an "export support institution"), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may separately designate export support institutions for the field of support for fishery food exports and the field of support for overseas certification of fishery food.
(3) Before designating an export support institution under Article 12 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall publicly announce the application period for designation and other matters necessary for applying for designation.
(4) An institution, organization, or corporation seeking designation as an export support institution shall submit to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries an application for designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, accompanied by the following documents:
1. The current status of personnel;
2. A plan for implementing, operating, and managing its business;
3. Its record of activities in the field of support for fishery food exports or support for overseas certification of fishery food;
4. Its articles of incorporation or equivalent business operating regulations.
(5) Upon designating an export support institution under Article 12 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall issue a certificate of designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(6) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries designates an export support institution under Article 12 (1) of the Act or revokes the designation of an export support institution under Article 12 (4) of the Act, the Minister shall publicly announce that fact.
(7) By January 31 each year, an export support institution shall report to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries its business plan for the relevant year and its performance in the previous year for the applicable projects among those specified in the subparagraphs of Article 12 (2) of the Act.
(8) The criteria for revoking the designation of an export support institution under Article 12 (4) of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 3.
(9) Matters necessary for the designation and operation of export support institutions, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (8), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

CHAPTER III PROMOTION OF THE VITALIZATION OF THE FISHERY FOOD INDUSTRY

Article 10 (Formulation of Measures to Foster the Fishery Products Processing Industry)
"Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 6 of Article 14 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Matters necessary for the coordinated development of fishery products and the industry processing them;
2. Other matters the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems necessary to foster the fishery products processing industry.

Article 11 (Scope of Support for the Production of Processed Fishery Products, etc.)
(1) "Processed fishery products prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 15 (1) 1 of the Act means products falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Products processed using fishery products as at least 50 percent of their raw materials or ingredients;
2. Products that have undergone secondary or further processing using products falling under subparagraph 1 as at least 50 percent of their raw materials or ingredients;
3. In the case of processed products using fishery products and products processed therefrom together with agricultural products (including forestry and livestock products; hereinafter the same shall apply) and products processed therefrom as raw materials or ingredients, processed products in which the content of fishery products or products processed therefrom exceeds that of agricultural products or products processed therefrom;
4. Other products processed using fishery products as their principal raw materials or ingredients for which the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems support necessary.
(2) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 15 (1) 4 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Installation, improvement, and repair of facilities for manufacturing or processing the processed fishery products under Article 15 (1) 1 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "processed fishery products");
2. Research and education to improve the hygiene and quality of processed fishery products;
3. Development and production of brands, containers, and packaging designs for processed fishery products;
4. Promotional projects, including the holding of and participation in sales exhibitions, product evaluation events, and fairs for processed fishery products;
5. Development of sales networks, including the establishment of electronic commerce for processed fishery products;
6. Education and consulting for the development of sales networks for processed fishery products;
7. Other projects the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems necessary to foster the fishery products processing industry.
(3) Where necessary to support the production of processed fishery products, etc., the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may provide support to fishery food business operators' organizations, associations, etc. composed of persons eligible for such support, or may give priority to supplying them with fishery products purchased and stockpiled under Article 40 or 41 of the Act on the Management of and Support for the Distribution of Fishery Products.

Article 12 (Designated Uses for Sales of State-Owned Land by Private Contract)
Where the State sells, by private contract under Article 15 (6) of the Act, State-owned land leased to oyster farmers, etc. under paragraph (5) of that Article, the uses that may be designated shall be as follows:
1. Construction and use of workplaces for shucking oysters or hygienically treating oysters (meaning washing and packaging);
2. Installation and operation of oyster production and processing facilities registered under Article 74 (1) of the Agricultural and Fishery Products Quality Control Act.

Article 13 (Categories of Fishery Products Processing Businesses Subject to Reporting)
(1) "Categories of business prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 16 (1) of the Act means the following categories of business: <Amended on January 10, 2023>
1. Aquatic animal oil processing business: A business processing aquatic animal oil on land using aquatic animals as raw materials;
2. Freezing or refrigeration business: A business freezing or refrigerating aquatic animals or plants on land as raw materials: Provided, That the following cases shall be excluded:
(a) Where fish is processed into surimi (meaning fish cakes, etc. in the form of a paste made by grinding fish meat and adding salt) and frozen;
(b) A refrigeration business with a refrigeration capacity of less than five tons;
(c) Where products are stored in freezing or refrigeration facilities equipped for the purpose of selling them at the relevant business operator's place of business;
3. On-board processing business: A business processing aquatic animal oil using aquatic animals as raw materials on fishing vessels licensed for fishing under Article 40 or 43 of the Fisheries Act, or a business freezing or refrigerating aquatic animals or plants as raw materials on fishing vessels licensed for distant water fishing under Article 6 (1) of the Distant Water Fisheries Development Act;
4. Other processing businesses: A business processing aquatic plants as raw materials for fertilizer, thickening agents (meaning ingredients used to maintain the shape of food or to provide its texture or mouthfeel), or feed, or processing leather using aquatic animals as raw materials.
(2) A person intending to engage in any of the following fishery products processing businesses under Article 16 (1) of the Act shall file a report with the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries:
1. Fishery products processing businesses under paragraph (1) 1 or 2 (limited to those conducted in Busan Metropolitan City);
2. Among the on-board processing businesses under paragraph (1) 3, a business processing aquatic animal oil using aquatic animals as raw materials on fishing vessels licensed for fishing (limited to those conducted in Busan Metropolitan City);
3. Among the on-board processing businesses under paragraph (1) 3, a business freezing or refrigerating aquatic animals or plants as raw materials on fishing vessels licensed for distant water fishing.
(3) A person intending to engage in any of the following fishery products processing businesses under Article 16 (1) of the Act shall file a report with the Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or head of the Si, Gun, or autonomous Gu (hereinafter referred to as the "head of a Si/Gun/Gu"):
1. Fishery products processing businesses under paragraph (1) 1 through 3 (excluding the fishery products processing businesses specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (2));
2. Fishery products processing businesses under paragraph (1) 4.

Article 14 (Categories of Fishery Products Processing Businesses Subject to Administrative Dispositions and Criteria for Administrative Dispositions)
(1) "Categories of business prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause of Article 17 (1) of the Act, with the exception of its subparagraphs, means the categories of business specified in the subparagraphs of Article 13 (1).
(2) Detailed criteria for administrative dispositions under Article 17 (1) of the Act against persons who have reported a fishery products processing business shall be as specified in Annex 4.

Article 15 (Projects Eligible for Exchange and Cooperation Programs, etc.)
(1) Projects eligible for exchange and cooperation programs under Article 18(1) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Contract production and supply projects between fishery producers or their organizations and fishery food business operators;
2. Projects to establish systems for supplying fishery products as raw materials and food ingredients between fishery producers or their organizations and fishery food business operators, and related projects;
3. Publicity and education projects, including the holding of fairs, seminars, and exhibitions, to link the fishery food industry with the fisheries industry;
4. Other projects that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems necessary to strengthen the links between the fishery food industry and the fisheries industry.
(2) Expenses of exchange and cooperation programs that may be subsidized under Article 18(2) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Facility expenses necessary for exchange and cooperation programs and expenses relating to the storage and transport of food ingredients;
2. Expenses necessary for holding and participating in fairs, seminars, exhibitions, etc. to promote exchange and cooperation programs;
3. Expenses necessary for surveys and research to promote exchange and cooperation programs;
4. Expenses necessary for publicity and education projects to promote exchange and cooperation programs;
5. Expenses necessary for projects to establish information networks to promote exchange and cooperation programs.
(3) A person seeking a subsidy for expenses under Article 18(2) of the Act shall submit an application to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, accompanied by the following documents:
1. A business plan;
2. A detailed budget required for the project.
(4) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (3), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall decide whether to provide support based on the following criteria and notify the applicant of the result:
1. The necessity and feasibility of the project;
2. The impact of the project on strengthening the competitiveness of fishery products;
3. The impact of the project on the development of the fishery food industry.
(5) Details necessary for exchange and cooperation programs, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 16 (Support for Contracts for School Meal Ingredients, etc.)
(1) Where any of the following persons conducts an exchange and cooperation program concerning the supply, etc. of food ingredients for school meals with a person who produces fishery products or fishery food, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may subsidize the necessary expenses pursuant to Article 19(2) of the Act:
1. A school meal support center under Article 5(4) of the School Meals Act;
2. A cooperative under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Fisheries Cooperatives Act;
3. A fisheries corporation under subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Act on Fostering and Supporting Agricultural and Fisheries Enterprises;
4. A fishery food business operator that uses excellent fishery ingredients under Article 39(1) of the Act as raw materials and is recognized by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.
(2) Expenses that may be subsidized under Article 19(2) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Expenses necessary for surveys to ensure a stable supply of school meal ingredients through contract production and direct transactions;
2. Expenses necessary for surveys on the safety of school meal ingredients supplied through contract production and direct transactions.
(3) Details necessary for supporting the supply of food ingredients under contracts, etc., other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be determined by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 17 (Support for the Globalization of Traditional Fishery Food)
(1) Support under Article 22(2) of the Act shall be provided to the following persons: <Amended on March 25, 2025>
1. Persons conducting publicity, education, research projects, etc. for the purpose of globalizing traditional fishery food and Korean food culture;
2. Persons that have expanded or plan to expand overseas to globalize traditional fishery food and Korean food culture;
3. Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters designated under Article 25 of the Act;
4. Persons that have obtained quality certification for traditional fishery food under Article 29 of the Act;
5. Other persons that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems in need of support for the globalization of traditional fishery food and Korean food culture, or that are prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government.
(2) Where a person falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (1) undertakes any of the following projects, the State and local governments may subsidize all or part of the costs pursuant to Article 22(2) of the Act: <Amended on March 25, 2025>
1. Education, survey, and research projects for the globalization of traditional fishery food or Korean food culture;
2. Projects to publicize the excellence of traditional fishery food or Korean food culture;
3. Projects to establish fishery food stores, restaurants, experience centers, etc. overseas for the globalization of traditional fishery food or Korean food culture;
4. Projects to support the evaluation, certification, and consulting of Korean fishery food restaurants overseas;
5. Projects to hold and participate in fishery food fairs, exhibitions, etc. in Korea and abroad;
6. Projects to establish information networks for the globalization of traditional fishery food or Korean food culture;
7. Other projects that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems necessary for the globalization of traditional fishery food and Korean food culture, or that are prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government.
(3) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to provide support pursuant to paragraph (1)5 or (2)7, the Minister shall consult with the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies.
(4) Matters necessary for supporting the globalization of traditional fishery food, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government. <Amended on March 25, 2025>

Article 18 (Publication of Fishery Food Composition Tables and Provision of Information, etc.)
(1) At intervals prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall compile and publish, in the form of fishery food composition tables, etc., the results of nutrient analyses and surveys and research on nutritional quality characteristics conducted pursuant to Article 23(1) of the Act for fishery food, including fishery products.
(2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may provide the fishery food composition tables, etc. under paragraph (1) in various forms or conduct publicity and education activities so that the public can use them.

CHAPTER IV QUALITY CERTIFICATION OF FISHERY FOOD

Article 19 (Fields for Designation of Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters)
The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may designate Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters in each of the following fields pursuant to Article 25(1) of the Act:
1. The manufacture, processing, and cooking of traditional fishery food;
2. The manufacture, processing, and cooking of fishery food other than traditional fishery food.

Article 20 (Qualifications of Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters, etc.)
(1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may designate a person who meets any of the following qualifications as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master pursuant to Article 25(1) of the Act:
1. A person who has been continuously engaged in the manufacture, processing, or cooking of the relevant fishery food for at least 20 years;
2. A person who preserves the methods of manufacturing, processing, or cooking traditional fishery food in their original form and is capable of reproducing them in that form;
3. A person who has received training in the skills possessed by a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master from that Master for at least five years (two years where the Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master dies during the training) and has been engaged in that trade for at least 10 years.
(2) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to designate a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master pursuant to Article 25(1) of the Act, the Minister shall evaluate the following matters:
1. The traditional character of the manufacture, processing, or cooking of fishery food (limited to evaluations in the field under subparagraph 1 of Article 19);
2. The excellence of the manufacture, processing, or cooking of fishery food (limited to evaluations in the field under subparagraph 2 of Article 19);
3. The authenticity of the skill holder;
4. The experience and activities of the skill holder;
5. The ethical conduct of the skill holder;
6. The need to pass on and develop the skills and their value for protection;
7. The industrial potential of the relevant fishery food.
(3) Detailed criteria, evaluation methods, etc. for the designation of Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 21 (Procedures for Designation of Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters, etc.)
(1) By April 30 of each year, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall publicly announce the matters necessary for the designation of Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters under Article 25(1) of the Act, including application methods and procedures and designation criteria.
(2) A person seeking designation as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master shall apply to the Mayor/Do Governor for a recommendation for designation, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(3) Upon receiving an application for a recommendation for designation as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master under paragraph (2), the Mayor/Do Governor shall investigate the facts stated in the application through an on-site investigation, etc. and, if the applicant is deemed to meet the designation criteria, recommend the applicant's designation to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(4) Upon receiving a recommendation for designation of a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master under paragraph (3), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall seek the opinions of experts in the relevant field and review the applicant's suitability, and shall designate a person deemed to meet the designation criteria as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master following deliberation by the Central Fisheries and Fishing Villages Policy Deliberation Council under Article 8 of the Framework Act on Fisheries and Fishing Villages Development.
(5) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries designates a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master pursuant to paragraph (4), the Minister shall publicly announce the designation as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries. The same shall apply where the designation of a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master is revoked pursuant to Article 25(5) of the Act or is deemed terminated pursuant to Article 23.

Article 22 (Issuance of Certificates of Designation as Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters, etc.)
(1) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries designates a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master pursuant to Article 21(4), the Minister shall issue the designated person a certificate of designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(2) A person whose designation as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master has been revoked pursuant to Article 25(5) of the Act shall return the certificate of designation under paragraph (1) to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries without delay.

Article 23 (Termination of Designation as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master)
Where a person designated as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master dies, the designation shall be deemed terminated.

Article 24 (Reporting on Activities of Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters)
(1) A person designated as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master pursuant to Article 25(1) of the Act shall report to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries once each year on the person's activities concerning the manufacture, processing, cooking, and sale of products and the transmission of skills, pursuant to paragraph (3) of that Article.
(2) Matters necessary for reporting on activities under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 25 (Financial Support for Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters, etc.)
(1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may provide the following funds to Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters within budgetary limits pursuant to Article 25(4) of the Act:
1. Funds for facilities and the purchase of food ingredients necessary for the manufacture, processing, cooking, etc. of fishery food;
2. Funds necessary for projects to promote sales of and publicize fishery food, including the development of packaging designs and the holding of and participation in exhibitions, fairs, etc.;
3. Funds necessary for research and education projects for the restoration and transmission of skills and for the establishment and expansion of facilities for the restoration and transmission of skills;
4. Funds necessary for publishing books and holding seminars, presentations, etc. in Korea and abroad for the transmission of skills;
5. Incentive grants necessary for passing on and developing Korean fishery food;
6. Funds necessary for other activities recognized by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.
(2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may provide a fishery food master apprentice selected pursuant to Article 25(8) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "fishery food master apprentice") with funds necessary for the following matters within budgetary limits pursuant to paragraph (4) of that Article:
1. Participation in skills training conducted by a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master;
2. Research, publicity, and education concerning products of a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master, the holding of exhibitions, and participation in competitions, etc.;
3. Other activities for which the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems financial support necessary.
(3) A Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master seeking support under paragraph (1)1 through 4 and 6 shall submit to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries an application prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, accompanied by a business plan.
(4) A Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master seeking support under paragraph (1)5 shall submit to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries an application prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, accompanied by records of activities as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master during the preceding year.
(5) A fishery food master apprentice seeking support under paragraph (2) shall submit to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries an application prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, accompanied by the following documents:
1. A detailed statement of skills training received (including documents, photographs, video recordings, etc. recording the content of such training in detail);
2. Other documents concerning research, publicity, and education projects for the transmission of skills, the holding of exhibitions, details of participation in competitions and awards received, etc. (limited to persons with relevant records).
(6) Where a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master who was receiving financial support under paragraph (1) has died but has a fishery food master apprentice (limited to a person who meets the qualifications for a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master under Article 20(1)), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may provide the apprentice with the funds under paragraph (1)1 through 4 and 6 for one year.
(7) Upon receiving an application under paragraphs (3) through (5), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall decide whether to provide support by assessing its feasibility, appropriateness, etc. and notify the applicant of the result.
(8) By January 31 of the relevant year, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall publicly announce the recipients, duration, amount of financial support, selection methods, etc. for support under paragraphs (1) through (7).
(9) Matters necessary for financial support, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (8), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 26 (Recovery of Subsidies and Suspension of Payment)
(1) "Grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as where a person is found to have received financial support by improper means, including the submission of false documents" in Article 25(6) of the Act means any of the following grounds:
1. Where a person has received financial support by fraud or improper means;
2. Where a person has received duplicate financial support under other statutes or regulations for the same or similar activities or projects.
(2) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to recover funds pursuant to Article 25(6) of the Act, the Minister shall give written notice of the grounds for recovery, the amount to be recovered, the payment deadline, the institution to which payment is to be made, the method of payment, etc. In such cases, the payment deadline shall be set at least 30 days after the date of notification of the recovery disposition.
(3) "Grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 25(9) of the Act means the revocation of designation as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master pursuant to paragraph (5) of that Article.
(4) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to suspend payment of subsidies pursuant to Article 25(9) of the Act, the Minister shall inform the person concerned in advance of the grounds for and timing of the suspension.

Article 27 (Recommendation and Selection of Fishery Food Master Apprentices)
(1) A Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master intending to recommend a fishery food master apprentice pursuant to Article 25(8) of the Act shall submit a letter of recommendation to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall select fishery food master apprentices from among persons recommended under paragraph (1), taking into account whether they are engaged in a field of skills or technology relating to the manufacture, processing, or cooking of fishery food, their level of skills, their potential for development, etc.
(3) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries selects a fishery food master apprentice pursuant to paragraph (2), the Minister shall issue the apprentice a certificate of selection prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(4) A person selected as a fishery food master apprentice pursuant to paragraph (2) shall acquire, record, and preserve the methods of manufacturing, processing, cooking, etc. of fishery food possessed by the Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master so that the distinctive skills of the relevant field can be passed on. In such cases, the Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master shall check the apprentice's activities concerning the acquisition, recording, and preservation of those skills.
(5) Matters necessary for the recommendation and selection of fishery food master apprentices, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 28 (Matters Subject to Inspection of Activities, etc.)
(1) Matters subject to inspection of activities under Article 26(1) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Whether the manufacturing, processing, cooking, etc. for which a person was designated as a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master pursuant to Article 25(1) of the Act can be reproduced;
2. Whether the activities of a Republic of Korea Fishery Food Master reported pursuant to Article 25(3) of the Act are true;
3. The status of management and operation of funds provided to Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters and fishery food master apprentices pursuant to Article 25(4) of the Act.
(2) Inspections of activities under paragraph (1) shall be conducted through on-site visits or by obtaining and examining relevant documents or the relevant products for which designation was granted.
(3) Details concerning inspections of activities, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 29 (Items Eligible for Industrial Standards Certification of Fishery Food, etc.)
(1) Items eligible for industrial standards certification of fishery food under Article 27 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "industrial standards certification") shall be processed fishery food.
(2) Industrial standards certification of processed fishery food under paragraph (1) shall be conducted by a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc. designated pursuant to Article 31(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc."), and matters concerning the criteria, labeling methods, application procedures, etc. for industrial standards certification shall be governed by the Industrial Standardization Act.

Article 30 (Quality Certification of Traditional Fishery Food)
(1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall designate items eligible for quality certification of traditional fishery food under Article 29 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "quality certification"), taking into account the following matters:
1. The item shall have traditional character and public appeal;
2. The item shall be capable of securing market competitiveness when commercialized;
3. The item shall be necessary for the preservation, succession, and development of traditional fishery food.
(2) For the efficient implementation of the quality certification system, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall establish standard specifications for the items designated as eligible for quality certification under paragraph (1).
(3) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries newly establishes, changes, or abolishes items eligible for quality certification and their standard specifications under paragraphs (1) and (2), the Minister shall publicly notify the details thereof.
(4) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may commission an institution prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries to undertake matters concerning the establishment, etc. of standard specifications for items eligible for quality certification.

Article 31 (Procedures for Applying for Quality Certification, etc.)
(1) A fishery food business operator producing an item eligible for quality certification under Article 30(1) who intends to obtain quality certification pursuant to Article 29(1) of the Act shall submit an application prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries for each item to the head of a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc.
(2) Upon receiving an application under paragraph (1), the head of a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc. shall conduct an examination in accordance with the standard specifications for items eligible for quality certification under Article 30(2) and the examination methods for quality certification prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, and shall grant quality certification where the item is determined to meet the examination criteria.
(3) Where the head of a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc. grants quality certification pursuant to paragraph (2), the head shall issue a certificate to the applicant for that certification, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 32 (Method of Indicating Quality Certification)
Where a person who has obtained quality certification under Article 31 intends to indicate such certification, the person shall affix or print the quality certification mark and labeling particulars prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries on the surfaces of the products, packaging, or containers for the certified item or on invoices, etc.

Article 33 (Procedures for Origin Certification of Processed Fishery Food and Restaurants, etc.)
(1) Where a person intends to obtain origin certification of processed fishery food or restaurants, etc. (referring to food service businesses under Article 36 (1) 3 of the Food Sanitation Act and institutional foodservice facilities under subparagraph 12 of Article 2 of that Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) pursuant to Article 30 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "origin certification"), a fishery food business operator producing processed fishery food shall apply for origin certification for each item, and an operator of a restaurant, etc. shall apply for origin certification for each type of business, to the head of the certification institution specified in the following subparagraphs:
1. Where the raw materials of the relevant processed food or the ingredients used in the relevant restaurant, etc. are fishery products, or the proportion of fishery products is higher than that of agricultural products: The head of a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc.;
2. Where the proportion of agricultural products among the raw materials of the relevant processed food or the ingredients used in the relevant restaurant, etc. is higher than that of fishery products: The head of a certification institution for excellent food, etc. designated by the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs pursuant to Article 24 (1) of the Food Industry Promotion Act (hereinafter referred to as a "certification institution for excellent food, etc.");
3. Where the proportions of fishery products and agricultural products among the raw materials of the relevant processed food or the ingredients used in the relevant restaurant, etc. are equal: The head of a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc. or the head of a certification institution for excellent food, etc., as applied for by the fishery food business operator or the operator of the restaurant, etc.
(2) Where a fishery food business operator or an operator of a restaurant, etc. intends to apply for origin certification to the head of a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc. pursuant to paragraph (1) 1 or 3, the operator shall submit an application prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(3) The head of a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc. who receives an application under paragraph (2) shall conduct an examination in accordance with the methods of certification examination prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries and shall grant origin certification if the subject of the application is determined to meet the certification criteria under Article 34.
(4) Where the head of a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc. grants origin certification under paragraph (3), the head shall issue a certificate prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries to the person who applied for the certification.
(5) Where an application for origin certification is filed with the head of a certification institution for excellent food, etc. pursuant to paragraph (1) 2 or 3, the methods and procedures for examination for such origin certification, etc. shall be governed by Article 31 of the Enforcement Decree of the Food Industry Promotion Act.

Article 34 (Criteria for Origin Certification)
(1) The criteria for origin certification under Article 30 (1) of the Act shall be as follows, according to the relevant category:
1. In the case of origin certification of processed fishery food, all of the following requirements shall be met:
(a) At least 95 percent of the raw materials, based on the proportions of raw materials blended in the product, shall have the same origin;
(b) Each raw material used in the product (excluding raw materials accounting for less than five percent of the proportions of raw materials blended in the product) shall have a uniform origin;
(c) Records shall be maintained so that origin information can be verified for all raw materials used in the product;
2. In the case of origin certification of a restaurant, etc., all of the following requirements shall be met:
(a) At least 95 percent of all ingredients used in the restaurant, etc. shall have the same origin;
(b) Each ingredient used in the restaurant, etc. (excluding ingredients accounting for less than five percent of all ingredients) shall have a uniform origin;
(c) Records shall be maintained so that origin information can be verified for all ingredients used in the restaurant, etc.
(2) The criteria under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) and other details necessary for origin certification shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 35 (Method of Indicating Origin Certification)
(1) Where a fishery food business operator producing processed fishery food that has obtained origin certification intends to indicate such certification, the operator shall affix or print the certification mark and labeling particulars prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries on the surfaces of the products, packaging, or containers for the certified item, etc.
(2) Where an operator of a restaurant, etc. that has obtained origin certification intends to indicate such certification, the operator shall affix the certification mark and labeling particulars prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries or display a plaque on the signboard or menu of the certified restaurant, etc. or in a readily visible place inside or outside the restaurant.

Article 36 (Matters to Be Reported by Certification Institutions for Excellent Fishery Food, etc.)
The head of a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc. shall report the following matters to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries and the Director-General of the National Fishery Products Quality Management Service within 20 days after the end of each quarter:
1. The status of industrial standard certification of fishery food under Article 27 of the Act;
2. The status of quality certification of traditional fishery food under Article 29 of the Act;
3. The status of origin certification under Article 30 of the Act;
4. The status of acceptance of reports on succession under Article 37 of the Act.

Article 37 (Reporting on Revocation of Certification of Excellent Fishery Food, etc.)
(1) Where the head of a certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc. revokes certification of excellent fishery food, etc. pursuant to Article 36 (1) of the Act, the head shall report such fact to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries without delay.
(2) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries revokes certification of excellent fishery food, etc. pursuant to Article 36 (1) of the Act, the Minister shall notify the head of the certification institution for excellent fishery food, etc. of such fact.

Article 38 (Fishery Food Eligible for Preferential Purchase)
"Fishery food certified under a national certification system prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 5 of Article 38 of the Act means the following fishery food:
1. Fishery food that has obtained industrial standard certification under Article 27 of the Act;
2. Fishery food produced at establishments applying food safety management certification standards under Article 48 (3) of the Food Sanitation Act;
3. Fishery products that have obtained quality certification under Article 14 of the Agricultural and Fishery Products Quality Control Act;
4. Fishery products or processed fishery products for which a geographical indication has been registered under Article 32 of the Agricultural and Fishery Products Quality Control Act;
5. Fishery products registered for traceability management under Article 27 of the Act on the Management and Support for the Distribution of Fishery Products.

Article 39 (Scope of Excellent Fishery Ingredients)
"Fishery food certified under a national certification system prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 39 (1) 9 of the Act means the following fishery food:
1. Fishery food that has obtained industrial standard certification under Article 27 of the Act;
2. Fishery products registered for traceability management under Article 27 of the Act on the Management and Support for the Distribution of Fishery Products;
3. Food or health functional food registered for traceability management under Article 49 of the Food Sanitation Act or Article 22-2 of the Health Functional Foods Act that constitutes fishery food.

Article 40 (Promotion of Use of Excellent Fishery Ingredients)
(1) Persons eligible for support under policies to promote the use of excellent fishery ingredients under the subparagraphs of Article 39 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "excellent fishery ingredients") shall, pursuant to paragraph (2) of that Article, be those who use excellent fishery ingredients among the following persons:
1. Enterprises that directly operate institutional foodservice facilities under subparagraph 12 of Article 2 of the Food Sanitation Act or are entrusted with operating such facilities;
2. Persons who produce processed fishery products;
3. Fishery food business operators recognized by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.
(2) Where a person falling under paragraph (1) implements any of the following projects, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may subsidize all or part of the costs within budgetary limits:
1. Projects to purchase excellent fishery ingredients;
2. Projects to supply excellent fishery ingredients for school meals, etc.;
3. Projects to establish a supply system for excellent fishery ingredients;
4. Other projects that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems necessary to promote the use of excellent fishery ingredients.
(3) Matters necessary to promote the use of excellent fishery ingredients, including procedures for support under policies to promote their use, other than those provided for in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be determined by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

CHAPTER V SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 41 (Delegation and Entrustment of Authority, etc.)
(1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall delegate the following authority to the President of the National Institute of Fisheries Science pursuant to Article 43 (1) of the Act:
1. Projects such as developing and disseminating varieties suitable for fishery food processing and providing education on aquaculture technology under Article 20 of the Act;
2. Providing information and preparing and disseminating guidelines and providing guidance thereon under Article 21 (1) of the Act;
3. Developing and disseminating menus and cooking methods under Article 21 (2) of the Act;
4. Providing education necessary for cooking and processing traditional fishery food, etc. under Article 21 (3) of the Act;
5. Analyzing the nutritional components of fishery food, publishing fishery food composition tables, and conducting surveys and research on the nutritional quality characteristics of fishery food, etc. under Article 23 (1) of the Act;
6. Receiving reports on the activities of Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters under Article 25 (3) of the Act;
7. Inspecting the activities of Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters and conducting follow-up management of products of Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters under Article 26 of the Act;
8. Providing and publicizing fishery food composition tables, etc. and providing education thereon under Article 18 (2);
9. Reviewing conformity under Article 21 (4);
10. Gathering expert opinions under Article 21 (6).
(2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall delegate the following authority to the Director-General of the National Fishery Products Quality Management Service pursuant to Article 43 (1) of the Act:
1. Accepting reports on fishery products processing businesses under Article 16 (1) of the Act (limited to on-board processing businesses under Article 13 (2) 3);
2. Restricting the items produced by fishery products processing businesses, suspending their business operations, closing their business premises, and taking measures to close their business premises under Article 17 (1) and (2) of the Act (limited to cases involving on-board processing businesses under Article 13 (2) 3);
3. Operating the quality certification system for traditional fishery food under Article 29 of the Act;
4. Operating the origin certification system for processed fishery food and restaurants, etc. under Article 30 of the Act;
5. Designating and redesignating certification institutions for excellent fishery food, etc. under Article 31 (1) and (3) of the Act;
6. Revoking the designation of certification institutions for excellent fishery food, etc. and suspending their business operations under Article 32 of the Act;
7. Conducting follow-up management of certification of excellent fishery food, etc. and certification institutions for excellent fishery food, etc. under Article 34 of the Act;
8. Issuing orders to change the labeling of, suspend the use of, or suspend the sale of fishery food that has obtained certification of excellent fishery food, etc. under Article 35 of the Act;
9. Revoking certification of excellent fishery food, etc. under Article 36 of the Act;
10. Accepting and receiving reports on succession to the status of certification institutions for excellent fishery food, etc. under Article 37 (2) of the Act;
11. Conducting hearings under subparagraphs 4 (limited to cases involving on-board processing businesses under Article 13 (2) 3 of this Decree), 6, and 7 of Article 42 (1) of the Act;
12. Imposing and collecting administrative fines under Article 47 (1) 1 of the Act (limited to cases involving on-board processing businesses under Article 13 (2) 3 of this Decree), subparagraphs 4 through 6 of the same paragraph, and paragraph (3) of the same Article (limited to cases involving on-board processing businesses under Article 13 (2) 3 of this Decree);
13. Designating items subject to quality certification; establishing standard specifications for items subject to quality certification; publicly notifying the addition, modification, or abolition of items subject to quality certification and their standard specifications; and referring matters concerning the establishment of standard specifications, etc. under Article 30.
(3) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall delegate authority to select recipients of financial support and to provide financial support under Article 15 (1) 3 of the Act to Mayors/Do Governors pursuant to Article 43 (1) of the Act.
(4) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall delegate the following authority to the Mayor of Busan Metropolitan City pursuant to Article 43 (1) of the Act:
1. Accepting reports on fishery products processing businesses under Article 16 (1) of the Act (limited to fishery products processing businesses under Article 13 (2) 1 and 2);
2. Restricting the items produced by fishery products processing businesses, suspending their business operations, closing their business premises, and taking measures to close their business premises under Article 17 (1) and (2) of the Act (limited to cases involving fishery products processing businesses under Article 13 (2) 1 and 2);
3. Conducting hearings under Article 42 (1) 4 of the Act (limited to cases involving fishery products processing businesses under Article 13 (2) 1 and 2);
4. Imposing and collecting administrative fines under Article 47 (1) 1 and (3) of the Act (limited to cases involving fishery products processing businesses under Article 13 (2) 1 and 2).
(5) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall entrust the affairs of fostering professionals for the fishery food industry under Article 6 (1) of the Act to the Korea Fisheries Infrastructure Public Agency established under Article 57 of the Fishing Villages and Fishery Harbors Act pursuant to Article 43 (2) of the Act.
(6) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall entrust the affairs of conducting statistical surveys on the fishery food industry under Article 7 of the Act to the Korea Maritime Institute established under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes pursuant to Article 43 (2) of the Act.

Article 42 (Processing of Unique Identification Information)
The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries (including persons to whom the Minister's authority or affairs are delegated or entrusted pursuant to Article 41) may process data containing resident registration numbers under subparagraph 1 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act where unavoidable for performing affairs concerning the provision and recovery of funds for Republic of Korea Fishery Food Masters and fishery food master apprentices under Article 25 (4) and (6) of the Act.

Article 43 (Review of Regulations)
The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall review the appropriateness of the following matters every three years, with January 1, 2022 as the base date (meaning before January 1 of every third year), and take measures for improvement, etc.:
1. Matters to be stated in the articles of association under Article 8 (1);
2. The scope of fishery food eligible for preferential purchase under Article 38.

